🔥 What is a Constructor in Java?

A constructor is a special method:

Used to initialize objects

Has the same name as the class

Does not have a return type

Automatically called when object is created

🔹 Example
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {   // Constructor
        this.name = name;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student("Prem");
        System.out.println(s1.name);
    }
}

👉 Constructor runs when new Student("Prem") is executed.

🔹 Types of Constructors

1️⃣ Default Constructor

Provided by Java if you don’t write any constructor.

2️⃣ Parameterized Constructor

Takes parameters to initialize object.

3️⃣ Copy Constructor (custom, user-defined concept)

🔥 Can a Constructor Be Private?

✅ Yes, a constructor can be private.

🔹 Why Use Private Constructor?
1️⃣ Singleton Design Pattern

Used when we want only one object of a class.

Example:

class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance = new Singleton();

    private Singleton() { }  // Private constructor

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return instance;
    }
}
